- venue: bioRxiv
- citation count: 14
[method]
The paper screened the 400-compound MMV Pathogen Box against intact T. gondii parasites using a Seahorse XFe96 extracellular flux assay to measure oxygen consumption. The screen was followed by fluorescence proliferation assays in T. gondii and P. falciparum, flow cytometry-based viability analysis, and human cell proliferation assays to rule out host-cell toxicity. This paper method is the closest analogue to the proposed method because it uses the same Pathogen Box and T. gondii phenotypic screening, but it used oxygen consumption as the primary tachyzoite readout and did not include mature bradyzoites or a 28-day regrowth endpoint.
[results]
Seven of the 400 Pathogen Box compounds were identified as inhibitors of O2 consumption in T. gondii. The identified compounds also inhibited proliferation and O2 consumption in P. falciparum. MMV688853 inhibited the electron transport chain in a TgCDPK1-independent manner and was assigned to the Qi site of Complex III. Atovaquone-resistant T. gondii and P. falciparum showed limited cross-resistance to most of the identified compounds, while ELQ-300-resistant T. gondii showed no cross-resistance to most of them. The supplied excerpt does not report numeric IC50, OCR fold-change, or p-values.
[context]
The assay was performed in 96-well Seahorse XFe96 format on intact T. gondii parasites with DMSO as the negative control; the excerpt does not specify bradyzoite culture, tdTomato-specific fluorescence, or a 28-day regrowth assay. The proposed method extends this tachyzoite oxygen-consumption screen to bradyzoites and uses tdTomato fluorescence plus inhibitor removal and 28-day tachyzoite regrowth. The supplied excerpt does not explicitly state the exact compound concentration or treatment duration, although the proposed method specifies 10 μM for 7 days; target follow-up used plasma membrane-permeabilized parasites and a Complex III enzymatic assay.
