The paper used mitochondrial isolation from Toxoplasma gondii followed by blue-native and clear-native PAGE, complexome profiling, SDS-PAGE and immunoblotting, and immunoprecipitation to resolve respiratory complexes and test whether candidate complex III, or cytochrome bc1, subunits co-migrate with an intact mitochondrial complex. Stable transfection and qRT-PCR were listed as methods for generating or monitoring parasite lines for candidate subunit analysis, and parasite growth plus JC-1 mitochondrial membrane potential staining were used as functional readouts.
[results]
Complexome profiling identified respiratory complexes and putative new subunits of complex IV and ATP synthase, an unusually large complex II with new apicomplexan subunits, and putative new complex III subunits. The candidate complex III subunits localized to the mitochondrion and were part of a ~670 kDa complex. The paper reported that novel complex III subunits were essential for parasite growth, mitochondrial membrane potential, and intact complex III. The supplied sections do not provide numerical fold changes, IC50 or LC50 values, statistical significance, metabolite abundance measurements, or stable isotope incorporation patterns.
[context]
The work was performed on Toxoplasma mitochondria using mitochondrial isolation, native and complexome PAGE, immunoblotting, immunoprecipitation, stable transfection, qRT-PCR, and JC-1 staining; the provided sections do not specify parasite stage, detailed buffers, antibody identities, gel systems, or statistical tests. This method relates to the proposed method because it characterizes the bc1/complex III target and mitochondrial membrane potential, the same functional axis tested by comparing atovaquone and buparvaquone with metabolomic and isotope signatures. It also aligns with the research plan’s mitochondrial function assays, such as MitoTracker Deep Red FM and ATP measurement, but it does not itself perform metabolite abundance or stable isotope tracing.
[end related work 1]

