  "contradictions": "No related work directly refutes the proposed method, but the strongest limitation is that the newer [(Souza et al., 2024-01)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f1939ffaa5cabdc13e6e11e10b46118f2d8e3eca), despite measuring atovaquone-treated metabolites, reports no quantitative changes in the HPLC-measured glycolytic or TCA metabolites; the only reported significant result was parasite counts at 7 days higher than at 24 h, 48 h, and 72 h, with p<0.05. This weakly contradicts the assumption that metabolite abundance alone will yield a clear atovaquone signature under the tested conditions, and it shows that a targeted HPLC/UV panel may be insufficient to validate the proposed untargeted LC-MS and isotope-resolved approach. It also used nM atovaquone and 24 h to 7 d time points rather than the proposed 3-5x IC50 for 3 h, so its conditions do not directly support the proposed acute comparative window. The weaker contradiction is that [(Maclean et al., 2021-03)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/3a60e8a80ac027f3facc33dc97725c12718e6943) does not measure metabolite abundance, stable isotope incorporation, IC50/LC50, fold changes, or statistical significance for membrane potential, and it relies on JC-1 rather than MitoTracker Deep Red FM; therefore it cannot validate the proposed isotope-resolved metabolomic confirmation of bc1 target engagement, leaving the central methodological claim unsupported by direct evidence.",
  "suggested_action": "Most important, generate matched positive-control datasets for atovaquone and buparvaquone under the exact proposed conditions, using 3-5x IC50 or LC50 concentrations, 3 h treatment, DMSO control, the same parasite isolation by mechanical disruption and filtration, acetonitrile extraction, pHILIC and BEH-amide LC-MS in positive and negative ionization modes, 13C6-glucose, and 15N-amide-glutamine, to define expected metabolite-abundance and isotope-incorporation signatures before classifying hits. Because [(Souza et al., 2024-01)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f1939ffaa5cabdc13e6e11e10b46118f2d8e3eca) shows that targeted HPLC/UV alone did not report quantitative metabolite changes, add orthogonal targeted HPLC/UV for the Souza metabolite panel, including phosphoenolpyruvate, pyruvate, lactate, citrate, malate, oxaloacetate, fumarate, succinate, alpha-ketoglutarate, and glucose, and include PMSF, rapid quenching, five independent biological replicates, GraphPad Prism 8.2.1 or equivalent, normality and homogeneous variance checks, ANOVA followed by Dunn's posttest, and p<0.05 for targeted comparisons. Next, strengthen target-engagement evidence by measuring mitochondrial membrane potential with MitoTracker Deep Red FM and, where feasible, JC-1 as in [(Maclean et al., 2021-03)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/3a60e8a80ac027f3facc33dc97725c12718e6943), ATP in glucose-starved parasites by luciferase assay, and TEM ultrastructure; if resources allow, add complex III integrity or bc1 subunit co-IP/complexome profiling to directly link inhibitor effects to intact bc1. Finally, use buparvaquone as a second bc1 reference, perform dose-response to confirm IC50/LC50, apply QC, normalization, PCA or OPLS-DA, fold-change thresholds, and multiple-testing correction for untargeted metabolomics, and require concordant abundance, isotope, and mitochondrial phenotypes to assign ETC/bc1 target.",
  "soundness_score": 6
}
